  ],
  "text": "The James Webb telescope looks in infrared, because the light from the first galaxies is not visible any more. It left as ordinary starlight. It arrives as something your eye cannot see. Because space has been expanding the entire time that light was travelling. The wave stretches along with it. Stretch visible light far enough and it becomes infrared. So to see the oldest things, you have to look in a colour they were never emitted in. The problem is that everything warm glows in infrared, including the telescope itself. So it sits behind a sunshield the size of a tennis court, built in five separate layers. That keeps the instruments at around forty degrees above absolute zero. And it orbits a point far out where the Earth and the Sun stay on the same side of it. A telescope that could not work anywhere warmer, chasing light stretched for thirteen billion years. One of these every day. Follow so you do not miss tomorrow.",
  "voice": "802e3bc2b27e49c2995d23ef70e6ac89",
  "model": "s2.1-pro-free"
}
